CH = dict(num=2, tag="History", theme="Non-Cooperation, Civil Disobedience and the many social groups in the freedom struggle",
 title="Nationalism in India",
 intro="Indian nationalism grew through the shared experience of colonial rule. Under Mahatma Gandhi the movement became a mass struggle that drew in peasants, workers, women, merchants and students. This chapter traces the path from the First World War to the Civil Disobedience Movement and shows how different groups understood freedom in different ways.",
 big_questions=["How did the First World War and Gandhi's arrival change the movement?", "What were the Rowlatt Act and Jallianwala Bagh?", "How did the Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience Movements differ?", "Why did some groups stay away or take part in their own way?", "How did a sense of collective belonging develop?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "The First World War, Khilafat and Non-Cooperation"),
 ("ul", ["The War (1914-18) created a new economic and political situation: huge defence expenditure was met by war loans and higher taxes; customs duties were raised and income tax introduced; prices doubled between 1913 and 1918, hurting the poor. Villages were forced to supply soldiers, and crop failures in 1918-19 and 1920-21 plus an influenza epidemic caused famine; about 12-13 million people died as per the 1921 census.", "People hoped that the hardships would end after the war, but they did not."]),
 ("h3", "The idea of Satyagraha"),
 ("p", "**Mahatma Gandhi** returned from South Africa in **January 1915**. His method of **satyagraha** stressed the power of truth and the need to search for truth. If the cause was true, physical force was not necessary; a satyagrahi could win through non-violence by appealing to the conscience of the oppressor."),
 ("table", ["Year", "Place", "Issue"], [["1916-17", "Champaran, Bihar", "Peasants forced by planters to grow indigo"], ["1917", "Kheda, Gujarat", "Peasants could not pay revenue after crop failure and plague"], ["1918", "Ahmedabad", "Cotton mill workers' demand for a wage increase"]]),
 ("h3", "The Rowlatt Act (1919)"),
 ("p", "In **1919** the Imperial Legislative Council passed the **Rowlatt Act** in spite of unanimous opposition from Indian members. It gave the government enormous powers to repress political activities and allowed detention of political prisoners without trial for two years. Gandhi called for a nationwide **hartal on 6 April 1919**. Rallies were held in cities; railway workshops shut down and shops closed. Alarmed, the British arrested local leaders and Gandhi was barred from Delhi. On **10 April** in Amritsar police fired on a peaceful procession, leading to violence. Martial law was imposed under **General Dyer**."),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Jallianwala Bagh, 13 April 1919.** A crowd gathered in an enclosed ground, many to attend the Baisakhi fair and some unaware of martial law. Dyer blocked the exit and ordered troops to fire without warning. Hundreds were killed. Dyer later said his aim was to produce a ‘moral effect’. People in Punjab were then crawled on streets, flogged and had villages bombed. Gandhi, shocked by the violence, called off the Rowlatt satyagraha."),
 ("h3", "Khilafat"),
 ("p", "After the war the Ottoman Empire's Sultan (the Khalifa, spiritual head of Muslims) was treated harshly by the peace treaty. To defend the Khalifa's temporal powers, a **Khilafat Committee** was formed in Bombay in March 1919. Leaders like **Muhammad Ali and Shaukat Ali** wanted a united national action; Gandhi saw it as a chance to bring Hindus and Muslims together in a mass movement. At the **Calcutta session of the Congress (September 1920)** he convinced other leaders of the need to start a non-cooperation movement in support of Khilafat and swaraj."),
 ("h3", "Non-Cooperation Movement (1920-22)"),
 ("p", "In his book *Hind Swaraj* (1909) Gandhi declared that British rule was established with the help of Indians and would collapse if Indians refused to cooperate. The Congress session at **Nagpur (December 1920)** adopted the programme."),
 ("ul", ["**Stages:** surrender of titles; boycott of civil services, army, police, courts, legislative councils, schools and foreign goods; and if the government used repression, a full civil disobedience campaign.", "**Impact:** Students left government schools, lawyers (Motilal Nehru, C. R. Das, Asaf Ali and others) gave up practice. Councils were boycotted except in Madras where the Justice Party, a party of non-Brahmans, felt that entering the council was a way to gain power. Foreign cloth was burnt, imports of foreign cloth halved between 1921 and 1922, and Indian textile mills and handlooms revived. Khadi was promoted.", "**In the countryside:** In **Awadh** peasants led by **Baba Ramchandra** fought against talukdars and landlords; the Oudh Kisan Sabha was set up in 1920 with Jawaharlal Nehru, Baba Ramchandra and others. In the **Gudem Hills of Andhra** a militant guerrilla movement under **Alluri Sitaram Raju** claimed Gandhi's name, though the forest-ban issues and violence were not Gandhian. **Plantation workers in Assam** left plantations, believing Gandhi Raj was coming.", "**End:** In **February 1922** a crowd at **Chauri Chaura (Gorakhpur, UP)** set a police station on fire, killing 22 policemen. Gandhi, committed to non-violence, withdrew the movement."]),
 ("h2", "Towards Civil Disobedience"),
 ("ul", ["Within the Congress, **C. R. Das and Motilal Nehru** formed the **Swaraj Party (1923)** to return to the councils; younger leaders like **Jawaharlal Nehru and Subhas Chandra Bose** pressed for radical mass agitation.", "Global depression, falling agricultural prices and rising unrest in the late 1920s added to the tension.", "The **Simon Commission**, set up in Britain to look into the constitutional system, had no Indian member. It arrived in India in **1928** and was greeted with ‘Go back Simon’ slogans.", "Lord Irwin announced a vague offer of ‘dominion status’ in October 1929 without any timeframe.", "At the **Lahore session (December 1929)** under Jawaharlal Nehru the Congress demanded **Purna Swaraj** (complete independence). **26 January 1930** was celebrated as Independence Day."]),
 ("h3", "The Salt March and Civil Disobedience"),
 ("p", "Gandhi found in **salt** a powerful symbol that could unite the nation: everyone used it and the government had a tax monopoly on its production. After a letter of eleven demands to Irwin was ignored, Gandhi began the **Salt March** on **12 March 1930** with 78 trusted volunteers from **Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi**, about 240 miles in 24 days. On **6 April** he reached Dandi and ceremonially violated the law by making salt from seawater. This began the **Civil Disobedience Movement**."),
 ("table", ["", "Non-Cooperation", "Civil Disobedience"], [["Idea", "Refuse to cooperate with the government", "Break colonial laws openly"], ["Period", "1920-22", "1930-34 (with a pause in 1931)"], ["Trigger", "Rowlatt Act, Jallianwala, Khilafat", "Salt tax, Purna Swaraj demand"], ["Included", "Boycott of schools, courts, councils, cloth", "Salt law broken, boycott, refusal of revenue and chaukidari tax, forest laws"], ["Ended by", "Chauri Chaura, 1922", "Gandhi-Irwin Pact 1931; resumed; slowed by 1934"]]),
 ("ul", ["Thousands in different areas broke salt law, boycotted foreign cloth and picketed liquor shops. Peasants refused to pay revenue and chaukidari taxes, and village officials resigned. Forest people violated forest laws.", "Alarmed, the colonial government arrested Congress leaders; when Gandhi was arrested in April 1930 angry crowds attacked police posts in Sholapur.", "In **March 1931** the **Gandhi-Irwin Pact** ended the movement; Gandhi agreed to attend the **Second Round Table Conference** in London (December 1931). The talks broke down, and Civil Disobedience was resumed; by 1934 it had lost momentum."]),
 ("h2", "How Participants Saw the Movement"),
 ("table", ["Group", "Role and aim"], [["Rich peasants (Patidars of Gujarat, Jats of UP)", "Hit by trade depression and fall in prices; fought high revenue demand; disappointed when the movement was called off without revenue being reduced"], ["Poor peasants", "Wanted rent to landlords to be remitted; Congress was unwilling to support ‘no rent’ campaigns for fear of upsetting landlords"], ["Business classes", "Indian Industrial and Commercial Congress (1920) and **FICCI** (1927); wanted protection against imports and a rupee-sterling ratio; shopkeepers refused to buy or sell foreign goods"], ["Industrial workers", "Nagpur workers participated; Congress was reluctant to include workers' demands"], ["Women", "Thousands marched, made salt and picketed; imprisoned; service seen as sacred duty but did not change their position at home"]]),
 ("h3", "Dalits and Muslims"),
 ("ul", ["Dalits, called ‘Untouchables’, were not part of the movement in some regions; Gandhi called them **Harijan** (children of God) and organised satyagraha for temple entry and access to public wells. Dalit leaders formed the **Depressed Classes Association (1930)**. **Dr B. R. Ambedkar** clashed with Gandhi over **separate electorates** at the Second Round Table Conference. When the British conceded, Gandhi began a fast unto death; Ambedkar accepted the **Poona Pact (September 1932)**, which gave the Depressed Classes reserved seats in provincial and central councils but voted in by the general electorate.", "After the Khilafat issue faded, many Muslims felt alienated from Congress. The Hindu Mahasabha and the Muslim League grew; **Muhammad Ali Jinnah** demanded reserved seats in a federal setup. Efforts at compromise broke down in 1927-28."]),
 ("h2", "The Sense of Collective Belonging"),
 ("ul", ["Nationalism spreads when people believe they belong to one nation. Unity was created by **history, fiction, folklore, songs, popular prints and symbols**.", "The image of **Bharat Mata** was first created by **Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay**, who wrote *Vande Mataram* as a hymn in the novel *Anandamath*; **Abanindranath Tagore** painted the famous image of Bharat Mata as an ascetic figure.", "Indian folklore was revived: **Rabindranath Tagore** collected ballads and nursery rhymes; Natesa Sastri published the *Folklore of Southern India*.", "A **tricolour flag** (red, green and white) with a spinning wheel was designed during the Swadeshi movement in Bengal; by 1921 Gandhi had designed a tricolour flag with the charkha, symbolising self-help.", "Reinterpretation of history helped; the British painted Indians as backward, and nationalist writers celebrated ancient greatness."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is satyagraha?", "A non-violent method of struggle based on truth and appeal to the conscience of the oppressor."),
  ("What was the Rowlatt Act?", "A 1919 law allowing detention without trial for two years."),
  ("What was the Khilafat issue?", "Protest against the harsh treatment of the Ottoman Khalifa after the First World War."),
  ("Why was the Non-Cooperation Movement stopped?", "Violence at Chauri Chaura in February 1922."),
  ("What was the Poona Pact?", "1932 agreement between Gandhi and Ambedkar giving Depressed Classes reserved seats without separate electorates."),
  ("Why did Gandhi choose salt?", "It was used by all, taxed by the state and could unite rich and poor."),
  ("Why did Indian industrialists support Civil Disobedience?", "They wanted protection from imports and a better currency ratio."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Gandhi's **satyagraha** turned the nationalist struggle into a mass movement.", "**Rowlatt Act and Jallianwala Bagh (1919)** and the **Khilafat** issue led to **Non-Cooperation (1920-22)**.", "**Civil Disobedience** began with the **Salt March (12 March - 6 April 1930)** and slowed after the 1931 pact.", "Each group, from Patidars and traders to Dalits and women, joined with its own hopes of swaraj.", "**Poona Pact (1932)** shows the tension between national unity and Dalit demands.", "Songs, images such as Bharat Mata and flags helped to build a sense of belonging."])
